13:/// A buffered line sink over [file]: truncates whatever a previous run
14:/// left (ONE trace per file — each armed run replaces the last trail;
15:/// collect across runs by copying the file between runs), then appends
16:/// lines in batches. Batching keeps the per-mark cost off the hot
17:/// stream path (a sync fsync per SSE chunk would perturb the very
18:/// cadence being measured): lines flush on a [flushInterval] timer,
19:/// when the buffer crosses [_kFlushThresholdBytes], or on
20:/// [flush]/[dispose]. The trade is explicit — up to one interval of
21:/// buffered lines is lost on a hard kill. Diagnostics contract: an IO
22:/// failure degrades to silence, never throws into the caller.
